cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "Cite the software version used and the associated RCC paper."
title: "rcc-refcert: a Python research toolkit for structure-fair quantum circuit complexity"
type: software
version: 0.3.0
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24248347"
url: "https://github.com/yeruciwenrou-dotcom/rcc-refcert/releases/tag/v0.3.0"
date-released: 2026-11-15
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/yeruciwenrou-dotcom/rcc-refcert"
abstract: >-
  rcc-refcert is a Python research toolkit for structure-fair quantum
  circuit complexity. Reference-Contingent Complexity (RCC) introduces
  structural fairness as a physical postulate, providing a common basis for
  resource accounting across physical backgrounds. Optimal quantum circuit
  complexity is the global minimum cost over all allowed preparation
  circuits and is generally difficult to compute. RCC provides a rigorous,
  auditable lower bound: measurements of the final state can rule out every
  preparation below a certified cost threshold. Researchers can
  independently verify this evidence without reconstructing the generation
  history or finding the optimal circuit.
  The toolkit implements finite-control models, reference certificates,
  terminal-state bounds, preparation-cost intervals and subsystem complexity
  profiles.
  Researchers can construct quantum-process models, verify supplied proof
  objects, and calculate lower bounds from exact spectra or predeclared
  fixed-projector counts. For exact diagonal-preserving models, the toolkit
  combines rigorous lower bounds with the costs of successful candidate
  programs to bound the optimal preparation cost. Window RCC compares subsystem
  complexity in pure and mixed states, analyzes local and correlation
  contributions under a common reference and control bandwidth, and follows
  these quantities along a preparation.
  Numerical model inputs support candidate diagnostics with their evidence
  level retained. Complete analysis bundles preserve inputs and historical
  results for independent recomputation. Python and command-line interfaces,
  three Jupyter tutorials, editable probes and reproducible reference cases
  connect these calculations to the RCC paper and to new research tasks.
keywords:
